problems.problem:
Determine a polynomial with integer coefficients that has

(a) the number $\sqrt2+\sqrt3$ among its zeros;

(b) both the numbers $\sqrt2+\sqrt3$ and $\sqrt2+\sqrt[3]3$ among its zeros.

For a real number $x$, let $\{x\}$ denote the difference between $x$ and the greatest integer $\le x$. Let $x_n$ and $y_n$ be two sequences of real numbers such that $\{x_n\}\to 0$ and $\{y_n\}\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$. Investigate which of the following conclusions are true, and justify your claims:

(a) $\{x_n+y_n\}\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$;

(b) $\{x_n-y_n\}\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$;

(c) $\{x_ny_n\}\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$.

In the constructions of geometry called Euclidean, compass and ruler may be used; the ruler is assumed to have one straight edge which can be used to draw the line through two given or already constructed points. In the constructions of this problem the compass may not be used, only a ruler. This ruler, however, has two parallel straight edges, and it may be used as in Euclidean constructions, to draw a line parallel to a given line at a distance from it equal to the width of the ruler, and to draw, through two given points at a distance at least equal to the width of the ruler, two parallel lines at distance equal to the ruler's width from each other, one through each of the points. Moreover, arbitrary auxiliary points may be chosen in the plane or on given lines. It is not allowed, however, to mark off segments by marking points on the edge of the ruler. Using these basic constructions, carry out the following constructions. Construct

(a) the bisector of a given angle;

(b) the perpendicular bisector of a given segment (note: there are two cases, depending on whether the segment is longer than the width of the ruler or not).

Consider the ``words'' that can be formed from the letters $a$ and $b$ by the following rules:

(i) $ab$ is a word;

(ii) if $X$ and $Y$ are words, then the concatenation $XY$ is a word (e.g. $abab$);

(iii) if $X$ is a word, then $aXb$ is a word (e.g. $aababb$).

Only words that can be formed by these rules are considered. How many different such words with $12$ letters are there?

Finitely many cells of an infinite square grid are coloured red. Prove that a number of squares with sides along grid lines can be drawn such that:

(1) no cell belongs to more than one square, although an edge may belong to more than one square;

(2) every red cell lies in one of the squares, and in each such square the proportion of red cells is at least $1/5$ and at most $4/5$.

Let $n\ge2$ be an integer. Prove that exactly $2^{n-3}n(n-1)$ integer $n$-tuples $(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n)$ satisfy:

(i) $a_1=0$;

(ii) for every $m$ with $2\le m\le n$, there is an index $i_m$ with $1\le i_m<m$ such that $|a_{i_m}-a_m|\le1$;

(iii) the tuple contains exactly $n-1$ distinct values.
